Notes for whoever packages this tree, written from the build on the author's bench
(Ubuntu 26.04 LTS "resolute", debhelper 13.31ubuntu1, dh-python 7.20260309,
python3-all 3.14.3-0ubuntu2, python3-setuptools 78.1.1-0.1build1, lintian
2.129.0ubuntu2.1, devscripts 2.26.7 - versions as reported by dpkg -l on 2026-11-07).

Format: 3.0 (native), and why
--------------------------------
This repository *is* the upstream: the protocol notes, the render code, the tools
and the vendor files kept as evidence all live in one tree, developed together, and
`debian/` sits in it as another part of that same work.  There is no separate
upstream release to diff an upstream tarball against, so a native source format is
the honest one; that also means no debian/watch file (there is no upstream tarball
url to point at) and no dfsg mechanism - which matters, because the vendor's files
are the part someone might want filtered out (next section), and with a native
format that decision has to be made by building the second binary package or not,
rather than by a repack rule.  If this is ever uploaded to a Debian-family archive,
expect to move it to 3.0 (quilt) with a repacked tarball, and expect the FTP masters
to ask about themes/alpha/ - debian/copyright answers the question rather than
leaving it to be found.

Version: debian/changelog's `0.1` is pyproject.toml's `0.1`, which is also the
version in the wheel's METADATA.  Three places, one number; keep them equal and
say so in the changelog when one moves.

Which bytes go in which package
-------------------------------
`xigmatek-connect-lcd` is the code plus what the code needs to look like the vendor
app out of the box: the two Python packages, the three console scripts
(`xigmalcd`, `xigmaui`, `xigmalcd-reset`), the three
background clips this project vendors (4.8 MB), the udev rule, the user unit, the
menu entry and the icon.

`xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes` is ALPHA's own library - the 27 `.thm` files as
shipped, their decrypted layouts, the clips, the baked overlay PNGs and the
manifest that hashes every one of them (145 MB).  Those bytes are XIGMATEK's and
nobody licensed them for redistribution here; debian/copyright's `OTHER` paragraph
says what was actually done to obtain them and why they are carried.  They are a
separate package so that the act of redistributing them is one deliberate choice at
install time, and so that removing them is one command: `xigmalcd/layout.py`, the
only code that reads the copied layouts, is used by tools/theme_check.py, which no
package installs, and the clips are scanned as one optional root by
xigmaui/clips.py.  Nothing reads them at startup, so the display loop keeps working
with the package absent.

tools/ is not installed by either package.  See debian/rules for the reason, and
README.md "If the panel goes white" for what a packaged system therefore does and
does not offer.

Building
--------
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b        # -us -uc: unsigned, see below
    lintian ../xigmatek-connect-lcd_*.changes

`--fail-missing` is on (override_dh_missing), so a file pybuild installs that no
.install file names is a build failure rather than something quietly dropped from
the package.

Signing: `-us -uc` means no .deb.signing/debsign signature on the .changes or the
.upload, and no Maintainers signature on the .buildinfo's signature block.  A GitHub
Release attachment carries no OpenPGP signature at all: authenticity there is HTTPS
plus the checksums the release body lists.  That is a real difference from an
archive upload and README says so where the .deb is offered.

Testing
-------
    sudo apt install ./../xigmatek-connect-lcd_0.1_all.deb
    debian/tests/smoke

debian/tests/smoke runs the checks that only make sense after an install: both
console scripts answering --help, the clip that pyproject.toml does not describe
being found under /usr/share/xigmalcd/media by the same root rules a checkout uses,
one frame rendered with no panel in the loop, the menu entry passing
desktop-file-validate, the udev rule and unit present with the unit holding no
--allow-reset, and (if the themes package is installed) every file in it hashing to
what manifest.json recorded.  It works under autopkgtest (`Tests: smoke` in
debian/tests/control, `Restrictions: superficial`) and by hand as above.

The offline suite, tools/selftest.sh, runs before packaging in CI and is the thing
to run after editing protocol.py, render.py or xigmaui/.  It is not run from
debian/rules' override_dh_auto_test: two of its sections read the author's bench
files under data/ (the Windows captures, the glass logs) and several measure wall
clock timings, so a build that failed because a runner was slow would be a build
failure that means nothing.
